    {
      "time": 1789585288.2863545,
      "body": "`pl_flare_baseline.py` is your copy \u2014 identical except it reads `batch[\"ground_truth\"]` (your scalar intensity) instead of `batch[\"forecast\"]`. Good to know: that's the 1D scalar path, so cell 34 currently wires up a scalar target.\n\nLet's go cell by cell.\n\n---\n\n### Cell 17 \u2014 this is your crash. Replace the whole cell:\n\n```python\n# HelioSpectformer2D has a long list of architecture arguments, and all of them come\n# straight from the model: section of the config. from_config() does that mapping, so the\n# backbone can never drift out of sync with the checkpoint it is about to load.\n#\n# Arguments that are not part of ModelConfig (dtype, the head's output shape, and anything\n# from the training: section) are passed as explicit overrides.\n#\n# ft_out_chans / ft_unembedding_type are the 2D head's knobs. There is no num_outputs:\n# that is a 1D concept (\"how many scalars to regress\"), and passing it is a TypeError.\nmodel = HelioSpectformer2D.from_config(\n    cfg.model,\n    ft_out_chans=len(cfg.data.channels),   # 13 -> output is (B, 13, 4096, 4096)\n    ft_unembedding_type=\"linear\",\n    dtype=cfg.dtype,\n    use_latitude_in_learned_flow=cfg.use_latitude_in_learned_flow,\n)\n```\n\n**Why:** `HelioSpectformer2D.__init__` has no `num_outputs` parameter, and `from_config` ends with `cls(**kwargs)` \u2014 so it dies binding arguments, before any model is built. The 1D head pools the 65536 tokens down to a scalar; the 2D head reshapes them into a 256\u00d7256 token grid and pixel-shuffles back up to a full image, so what it needs to know is *how many channels*, not how many scalars.\n\nAlso note `pooling: class_token`, `dropout`, `penultimate_linear_layer` in your YAML are **silently ignored** here \u2014 `from_config` for 2D never passes them. Not a problem, just don't expect them to do anything.\n\n---\n\n### Cells 28 / 30 / 32 \u2014 the target needs an axis dropped\n\n```python\n# batch[\"forecast\"] is (B, 13, 1, 4096, 4096) \u2014 the 1 is the lead-time axis.\n# The model outputs (B, 13, 4096, 4096), so drop it. Valid only because rollout_steps: 0\n# gives exactly one target frame.\ntarget = batch[\"forecast\"][:, :, 0].float()\ntrain_loss_metrics(output, target)\n```\n\u2026and the same `target` in 30 and 32. These need cells 13 and 23 uncommented to have `batch` and `output` in scope.\n\nYour `FlareMetrics` needs no changes \u2014 it already flattens both sides with `reshape(-1)`, so MSE and RRSE work on images as-is.\n\n---\n\n### Cells 34 + 38 \u2014 the 2D Lightning module\n\nCell 34's `FlareLightningModule` builds `target = batch[\"ground_truth\"].unsqueeze(1)` \u2014 your scalar intensity. For the image task, **keep cell 34 as-is** and add a new cell after it:\n\n```python\nclass Flare2DLightningModule(FlareLightningModule):\n    \"\"\"FlareLightningModule with a 2D image target instead of a scalar label.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _target(batch):\n        fc = batch[\"forecast\"]\n        assert fc.shape[2] == 1, (\n            f\"expected one target frame, got {fc.shape[2]}. Set training.rollout_steps: 0.\"\n        )\n        return fc[:, :, 0].float()          # (B, C, H, W)\n\n    def _step(self, batch, stage):\n        target = self._target(batch)\n        if self.preprocess_fn is not None:\n            batch = self.preprocess_fn(batch)\n        output = self(batch)\n\n        loss_fn = self.training_loss if stage == \"train\" else self.validation_loss\n        eval_fn = self.training_evaluation if stage == \"train\" else self.validation_evaluation\n\n        losses, weights = loss_fn(output, target)\n        loss = self._combine_losses(losses, weights)\n        self.log(f\"{stage}_loss\", loss, prog_bar=True, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n        for k, v in losses.items():\n            self.log(f\"{stage}_loss_{k}\", v, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n\n        mets, mw = eval_fn(output, target)\n        if len(mw) > 0:\n            for k, v in mets.items():\n                self.log(f\"{stage}_metric_{k}\", v, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n        return loss\n\n    def training_step(self, batch, batch_idx):   return self._step(batch, \"train\")\n    def validation_step(self, batch, batch_idx): self._step(batch, \"val\")\n```\n\nThen in **cell 38**, one word changes:\n```python\nlit_model = Flare2DLightningModule(model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size)\n```\n\n**Why:** the inherited `training_step` does `batch[\"ground_truth\"].unsqueeze(1)` \u2192 `(B, 1, 1)` against a `(B, 13, 4096, 4096)` output. Shape mismatch in `mse_loss`. Subclassing reuses the optimizer, `_combine_losses`, and all the logging.\n\n**Heads-up on your dataset:** `flare_dataset.py:142-143` sets `sample[\"ground_truth\"]` to the scalar intensity, but your comment right below says *\"ground truth is the image the model is training to predict.\"* Those disagree. The module above sidesteps it by using `forecast` (the actual image from helio.py). If you'd rather `ground_truth` *be* the image, say so and we'll change the dataset instead \u2014 but not both, or they'll fight.\n\n---\n\n### Cell 21 \u2014 no change needed, but check what it prints\n\nLook for `attn.qkv` / `attn.proj` in the `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list. If you only see `fc1`/`fc2`, that's the stale `target_modules` in your config (line 148) \u2014 attention isn't being adapted. Optional, doesn't crash.\n\n---\n\nWant to stop here and run 13 \u2192 17 \u2192 19 \u2192 21 \u2192 23 to confirm the model builds and the forward pass gives `(2, 13, 4096, 4096)`? Then I'll give you cell 19's decoder recovery and the plot cell, which are the two that actually make the picture look like the Sun.",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "cfcfa31e-0cbc-4a76-8337-35c1145b4629",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "body": "this was the output for cell 13, can you confirm the rest of my edits are good before i continue running? the batch commands take SO LONG to run\n{'ts': (2, 13, 2, 4096, 4096), 'time_delta_input': (2, 2), 'forecast': (2, 13, 1, 4096, 4096), 'lead_time_delta': (2, 1), 'ground_truth': (2,), 'ds_index': 'list', 'was_flipped': (2,)}",
