      "time": 1789586447.1011527,
      "body": "All verified without touching S3. Cell-by-cell verdict:\n\n| Cell | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| 13 | \u2705 shapes confirm the contract |\n| **17** | \u2705 **correct** \u2014 I built the real `HelioSpectformer2D` with your exact args; output is `(B, 13, IMG, IMG)`, so yours gives `(2, 13, 4096, 4096)` |\n| 19 | \u2705 runs (will report `157 / 159` \u2014 see below) |\n| 21 | \u2705 runs, and `head_unembed` **is** discovered \u2192 your head stays trainable under LoRA |\n| 23 | \u26a0\ufe0f correct but **will appear to hang** \u2014 see below |\n| 25 | \u2705 fixed |\n| 28 | \u2705 correct |\n| **30, 32** | \u26a0\ufe0f **not updated** \u2014 still `batch[\"forecast\"]` |\n| 34+35 | \u2705 correct |\n| 39 | \u2705 `Flare2DLightningModule` wired up |\n\n---\n\n### Cells 30 / 32 \u2014 fix them, but they're not what's biting you\n\n```python\ntrain_evaluation_metrics(output, target)        # cell 30\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics(output, target)   # cell 32\n```\n\nWorth knowing precisely: as written they **work by accident**. `FlareMetrics` flattens both sides with `reshape(-1)`, and the lead-time axis is size 1, so `2\u00d713\u00d71\u00d74096\u00d74096` and `2\u00d713\u00d74096\u00d74096` flatten to the *same* 436M elements in the same order. Identical numbers either way. It becomes a silent wrong answer the moment you set `rollout_steps > 0`. So fix it, but don't expect it to change today's output.\n\n### Cell 23 \u2014 this is your real time sink, not the dataloader\n\nYour model is still **on the CPU** at cell 23. A 4096\u00d74096 forward means 65536 tokens through 10 blocks plus a 13\u00d74096\u00d74096 decoder \u2014 on CPU that's not slow, it's effectively hung. Replace cell 23:\n\n```python\n# Move to GPU first: this forward is 65536 tokens through 10 blocks. On CPU it is\n# effectively unbounded. no_grad + autocast keeps activations from being stored.\nmodel = model.cuda()\nbatch_gpu = {k: (v.cuda() if torch.is_tensor(v) else v) for k, v in batch.items()}\nwith torch.no_grad(), torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n    output = model(batch_gpu)\nprint(output.shape, output.dtype)\n```\n\nThen cells 28/30/32 need the target on the same device:\n```python\ntarget = batch[\"forecast\"][:, :, 0].to(output.device, torch.float32)\n```\n(I checked \u2014 mixing bf16 predictions with an fp32 target is fine for both MSE and RRSE, so no cast needed on `output`.)\n\nAlso: cell 23's trailing comment `# Get rid of singleton dimension` is left over from the 1D version \u2014 nothing is squeezed now. And drop the bare `output` on the last line; echoing a 436M-element tensor is pointless.\n\nYou don't need to re-run cell 13 \u2014 cell 23 reuses `batch` from it. Delete the duplicate `batch = next(iter(train_data_loader))` line if it's still in 23, or you'll pay the S3 cost twice.\n\n---\n\n### On config line 148 \u2014 I measured it\n\nYour list adapts **4** modules; the patched list adapts **6**:\n\n```\nYOUR config:       fc1, fc2 (\u00d72 blocks)                          \u2192 4 modules\npatched template:  fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj                 \u2192 6 modules\n```\n\nSo attention genuinely never gets adapted \u2014 `q_proj`/`k_proj`/`v_proj`/`out_proj` match nothing. Still optional, still won't crash.\n\n---\n\nRun 17 \u2192 19 \u2192 21 \u2192 23 now. Two things to watch:\n- cell 19 prints `Loaded 157 / 159` \u2014 the 2 missing are the decoder, which is why your head is random\n- cell 23 should print `torch.Size([2, 13, 4096, 4096])`\n\nOnce those look right, tell me and I'll give you the decoder-recovery snippet plus the plot cell \u2014 that's the pair that turns the output from noise into an actual Sun."
    },
