      "body": "I couldn't run the toy fit (permission declined), so this is a static check against the parent class \u2014 which is sufficient here, since cell 35 only depends on names that either exist on `FlareLightningModule` or don't.\n\n## Cells 26\u201339: good to run\n\n**Cell 26** \u2705 \u2014 four modes, all valid `FlareMetrics` names.\n\n**Cells 28 / 30 / 32** \u2705 \u2014 all three now use `target`, and your outputs prove the chain works end to end:\n- `mse 1.1731` (with `grad_fn=<MseLossBackward0>`) \u2014 differentiable, so backprop will work\n- `rrse 1.1868`\n\n**Cell 35** \u2705 \u2014 every attribute your `_step` reaches for exists on the parent, at these lines in `lightning_modules/pl_flare_baseline.py`:\n\n| your code | parent |\n|---|---|\n| `self.preprocess_fn` | `:119` |\n| `self.training_loss` / `self.validation_loss` | `:122` / `:125` |\n| `self.training_evaluation` / `self.validation_evaluation` | `:128` / `:129` |\n| `self._combine_losses` | `:134` |\n| `self.batch_size` | `:117` |\n| `self(batch)` \u2192 `self.model(batch)` | `:162` |\n| `configure_optimizers` (inherited) | `:267` |\n\nThe reason this subclass is safe: you **fully override** `training_step` and `validation_step`, so the parent's `target = batch[\"ground_truth\"].unsqueeze(1).float()` (`:194` and `:239`) never runs. That line is the scalar path \u2014 it would give you `(2, 1)` against a `(2, 13, 4096, 4096)` prediction. It's now dead code on your path, and `1_baseline_template.ipynb` still gets it unchanged.\n\n**Cell 39** \u2705 \u2014 signature matches (`model, metrics, lr, batch_size=None, preprocess_fn=None`), `batch_size` is defined in cell 11, `cfg.learning_rate` resolves. This cell is just a constructor; it does no shape work and cannot fail on tensor shapes.\n\n**So: run 37 \u2192 39 \u2192 41 \u2192 43 \u2192 45 now.**\n\n---\n\n## Two things to watch in cell 45\n\n**1. Your cell 45 output is stale \u2014 from the 1D model.** It says `2.7 M Trainable params`. That's the 1D head (1,642,241) plus your `fc1`/`fc2`-only LoRA (1,024,000) = 2,666,241. When you re-run it with the 2D model you should see **~5.3 M**:\n\n```\nLoRA (fc1/fc2, 10 blocks)            1,024,000\nhead_unembed Conv2d(1280 -> 3328)    4,263,168   <- 3328 = 16\u00b2 \u00d7 13\n                                     ---------\n                                     5,287,168\n```\n\nIf it still prints 2.7 M, cell 17 didn't get re-run. That number is your cheapest confirmation that the 2D head is actually in the graph.\n\n**2. Memory.** You're on a 46 GB L40S, and at `batch_size: 2` the big residents are `ts` at 3.5 GB fp32, `forecast` at 1.7 GB, the `.float()` target copy at another 1.7 GB, and the fp32 upcast inside `mse_loss` at 1.7 GB more. My estimate is ~18\u201320 GB peak, so it should fit \u2014 but if it OOMs, the first lever is `training.batch_size: 1`, not anything structural.\n\nRelated: `train_metrics` computes RRSE over 436 M elements *every* training step. That's real time for a number you're only watching. If steps feel slow, pass `train_metrics` a mode that returns nothing, or just accept it for now \u2014 the S3 loads dominate anyway.\n\n---\n\n## The thing that will make your plots look like noise\n\nCell 19 reports **157 / 159**, and your `rrse = 1.1868` is the proof: RRSE above 1 means the prediction is *worse than just predicting the channel mean*. Your `head_unembed` is at random init.\n\nThe 2 missing tensors are Surya's own pretraining decoder, stored as `unembed.unembed.0.{weight,bias}` with shape `(3328, 1280, 1, 1)`. Because you set `ft_out_chans=13`, that is **bit-for-bit the `LinearDecoder` you just built**. `load_pretrained_weights` only tries the keys `k` and `backbone.{k}` (`workshop_infrastructure/utils.py:404`), never `head_{k}`, so it's silently dropped.\n\nYou can recover it without touching any shared file \u2014 **new cell between 19 and 21**:\n\n```python\n# The checkpoint also ships Surya's pretraining decoder under \"unembed.*\". Our head is\n# \"head_unembed\" (the head_ convention LoRA needs), a spelling load_pretrained_weights\n# never tries -- hence 157 / 159 and a random readout. With ft_out_chans=13 our\n# LinearDecoder is identical to that decoder, so copy it in by hand.\nckpt = torch.load(cfg.model.pretrained_path, weights_only=True, map_location=\"cpu\")\nsd = model.state_dict()\nrecovered = {}\nfor k, v in ckpt.items():\n    if not k.startswith(\"unembed.\"):\n        continue\n    new_k = \"head_\" + k                      # unembed.unembed.0.weight -> head_unembed...\n    assert new_k in sd and sd[new_k].shape == v.shape, (new_k, tuple(v.shape))\n    recovered[new_k] = v\nmodel.load_state_dict({**sd, **recovered}, strict=True)\nprint(f\"recovered {len(recovered)} decoder tensors: {list(recovered)}\")\n```\n\nIt must go **before cell 21** \u2014 once PEFT wraps the model the keys become `base_model.model.head_unembed.original_module.*` and this won't match.\n\nThe catch: inserting it means re-running 19 \u2192 21 \u2192 23, and cell 23 is your CPU forward pass at 4096 resolution. So I'd suggest: **run the training now** to prove the loop works, then do the decoder recovery as a second pass before you start caring about the plots. Once you tell me the fit completed, I'll give you the plot cell.Ordered, cheapest first:\n\n1. **`training.batch_size: 1`** in the YAML \u2192 re-run cells 5 and 11 only (S3 cache is warm, so this is fast). Halves every big tensor: ~20 GB \u2192 ~10 GB. This alone almost certainly fixes it.\n2. **Restart the kernel first.** You ran the 4096 forward in cell 23 and the old 1D model is probably still holding allocations. Free win.\n3. **Drop `train_metrics`** \u2014 in cell 39, set `'train_metrics': FlareMetrics(\"val_loss\")` is *not* what you want; instead just accept it, or if you need the memory, RRSE's transient `preds - target` at fp32 is ~1.7 GB per step at B=2, less at B=1.\n4. **Last resort: `ft_out_chans=1`** in cell 17, with `target = batch[\"forecast\"][:, :1, 0].float()` (channel 0 only). Cuts the decoder 13\u00d7. But you lose the pretrained decoder *and* `inverse_transform_data()` \u2014 which asserts 13 channels \u2014 so the plot cell won't work. Avoid unless 1 and 2 fail.\n\nStart with 2 then 1. Don't touch 4.Yes, it's correct \u2014 and you already have proof. Your cell 45 output shows:\n\n```\nwandb: Currently logged in as: jperei4 (surya-ws2) to https://api.wandb.ai\n```\n\nThat means `cfg.wandb_entity` resolved to the team org and `cfg.wandb_project` resolved fine. Nothing to change.\n\nNotes on the pieces:\n\n- **`log_model=False`** \u2014 keep this. Otherwise wandb uploads your 1.5 GB checkpoint on every improvement. `UploadBestCheckpointToS3` is the intended path for that, and `output.s3_bucket: null` means it's off.\n- **`save_dir=\"./jpereira/wandb/wandb_tmp\"`** \u2014 harmless, just local scratch for wandb's staging files. It gets created for you. Relative to the notebook dir, so it lands in `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/jpereira/...`. Add it to `.gitignore` if it isn't already covered.\n- **`CSVLogger(\"runs\", name=project_name)`** \u2014 writes `./runs/flare_flip_forecast_1/version_N/metrics.csv`. Version number auto-increments per run, so nothing overwrites. Good.\n\n**One thing I'd change:** `run_name` is a fixed string. wandb doesn't dedupe by name, so every re-run creates *another* run also called `flare_flip_finetune_test_1` and you won't be able to tell them apart in the UI \u2014 which is exactly when you need to, since you're about to compare before/after the decoder fix.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\nrun_name = f\"flare_flip_finetune_{datetime.now():%m%d_%H%M}\"\n```\n\nOr name them for the thing you changed: `\"randomhead_bs2\"` vs `\"pretrainedhead_bs2\"`. That's the comparison you actually care about.You imported the module, not the class. Change line 2:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\n```\n\n`import datetime` gives you the module, whose `now()` lives on `datetime.datetime`. So either use `from datetime import datetime` (what I wrote) or keep your import and write `datetime.datetime.now()`.`lit_model` is created in the **\"Initialize Lightning module\"** cell \u2014 the one with:\n\n```python\nmetrics = {...}\nlit_model = Flare2DLightningModule(model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size)\n```\n\nYou skipped it. `trainer` and `train_data_loader` resolved fine, so your kernel is alive \u2014 you just jumped from the trainer cell straight to `fit`.\n\nRun these in order, then re-run `fit`:\n\n1. the `FlareMetrics(\"train_loss\")` \u2026 cell (creates the four metric objects)\n2. `from ...pl_flare_baseline import FlareLightningModule`\n3. the `class Flare2DLightningModule(FlareLightningModule):` cell\n4. the `metrics = {...}` / `lit_model = ...` cell\n\nAll four are cheap \u2014 no S3, no forward pass. Then `trainer.fit(...)`.All three are harmless \u2014 the run is proceeding.\n\n- **`WARNING The anonymous setting has no effect`** \u2014 Lightning's `WandbLogger` passes `anonymous=None` through to wandb, which is deprecating that arg. Nothing you set, nothing to fix.\n- **`ERROR Failed to detect the name of this notebook`** \u2014 labelled ERROR but non-fatal. wandb can't auto-detect the filename in JupyterLab/VSCode, so it skips *code saving* (uploading the `.ipynb` as an artifact). Your metrics still log normally. If you want it, add to your first cell, before the wandb import:\n  ```python\n  os.environ[\"WANDB_NOTEBOOK_NAME\"] = \"2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb\"\n  ```\n- **`Currently logged in as: jperei4 (surya-ws2)`** \u2014 this is the good line. Authenticated to the team entity.\n\nWhat to watch for next: the model summary should print **~5.3 M trainable** (not 2.7 M), then sanity check \u2192 training. If it OOMs it'll happen within the first step or two.",
