              "command": "cd /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/flare_forecast && /home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python -c \"\nimport json\nnb=json.load(open('2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb'))\nfor i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']):\n    src=[l.rstrip() for l in c['source'] if l.strip() and not l.strip().startswith('#')]\n    head=' | '.join(src[:2])[:110]\n    print(f'{i:>3} {c[\\\"cell_type\\\"][:4]:>4}  {head}')\n\""
            },
            "raw_output": "  0 mark  by Andr\u00e9s Mu\u00f1oz-Jaramillo | This notebook is meant to act as a template to train and use a surya model to impl\n  1 mark  **IMPORTANT:** Since we are sharing resources, please make sure that the cuda visible device you put here is t\n  2 code  import os | os.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = \"0\"\n  3 code  import os | os.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")\n  4 mark  Surya was designed to read a configuration file that defines many aspects of the model | including the data it\n  5 code  from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.configs import load_flare_config | cfg = load_flare_config(\"./configs/conf\n  6 mark  The config says where the assets belong, so it is loaded first. `ensure_assets()` fetches only what is missing\n  7 code  from workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets | ensure_assets(cfg, which=[\"scalers\", \"weights\"])\n  8 mark  `load_flare_config()` reads `configs/config_script.yaml` and returns a typed `TrainingConfig`. | This notebook\n  9 mark  This child class takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus additonal parameters relevant to the dow\n 10 code  from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.datasets.flare_dataset import flare_Dataset\n 11 code  from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders | train_data_loader, val_data_lo\n 12 mark  Training and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in \n 13 code  batch = next(iter(train_data_loader)) | print({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \"shape\") else type(v).__name__\n 14 mark  This is the main difference beteween the notebook that trains the simple model and the one that fine-tunes Sur\n 15 code  from workshop_infrastructure.models.finetune_models import HelioSpectformer2D\n 16 mark  Now the config file really comes into bear. The Spectformer has a metric ton of hyperparameters\n 17 code  model = HelioSpectformer2D.from_config( |     cfg.model,\n 18 mark  Here we load the pre-trained checkpoint and load the weights.  The exercise of loading follows the idea of us \n 19 code  from workshop_infrastructure.utils import load_pretrained_weights | load_pretrained_weights(model, cfg.model.p\n 20 mark  This cell gives you two options.  On the one hand we have the classic freezing of the backbone (the initial la\n 21 code  if cfg.model.freeze_backbone: |     for name, param in model.named_parameters():\n 22 mark  We can now test that this model manipulates a batch as expected and returns an estimate of flare intensity as \n 23 code  batch = next(iter(train_data_loader)) | output = model.forward(batch)  # Get rid of singleton dimension\n 24 mark  Metrics are a very important part of training AI models.   They provide your models with the quantitification \n 25 code  from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.metrics.flare_metrics import FlareMetrics\n 26 code  train_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"train_loss\") | val_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"val_loss\")\n 27 mark  Now they can be evaluated in our model's output and our ground truth.   First the loss that actually will back\n 28 code  target = batch[\"forecast\"][:, :, 0].float() | train_loss_metrics(output, target)\n 29 mark  Then a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we can keep an eye on. N\n 30 code  train_evaluation_metrics(output, target)\n 31 mark  In the validation evaluation metrics we report both MSE and RRSE\n 32 code  validation_evaluation_metrics(output, target)\n 33 mark  In this workshop we will use PyTorch lightning to train our models.  PyTorch lighting reduces the amount of co\n 34 code  from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.lightning_modules.pl_flare_baseline import FlareLightningModule\n 35 code  class Flare2DLightningModule(FlareLightningModule): |     \"\"\"FlareLightningModule with a 2D image target inste\n 36 mark  Since training AI models generally uses stochastic gradient descent, it is a good idea to fix your random seed\n 37 code  L.seed_everything(42, workers=True)\n 38 mark  Now we properly initalize the Lightning module to enable training, including passing the dictionary of metrics\n 39 code  metrics = { |     'train_loss': train_loss_metrics,\n 40 mark  In order to properly compare experiments against each other, it is very useful to log evaluation metrics in a \n 41 code  project_name = cfg.wandb_project | from datetime import datetime\n 42 mark  With the loggers done, now the trainer needs to be defined.  The trainer defines several properties of your tr\n 43 code  max_epochs = 2 | trainer = L.Trainer(\n 44 mark  Finally we fit the model.  We pass the Lighting module, and our dataloaders.\n 45 code  trainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\n 46 mark  With this we have now integrated our dataset, dataloaders, metrics, and DS into an end-2-end training loop and\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/flare_forecast/old-chats-notebooks",
            "title": "cd flare_forecast && python -c \" import json nb=json.load(open('2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb')) for i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']): src=[l.rstrip() for l in c['source'] if l.strip() and not l.strip().startswith('#')] head=' | '.join(src[:2])[:110] print(f'{i:>3} {c[\\\"cell_type\\\"][:4]:>4} {head}') \"",
