      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "It probably wasn't the GPU. A CUDA OOM raises `torch.OutOfMemoryError` with a traceback \u2014 the kernel survives. A kernel that dies and needs a reconnect was **SIGKILLed, which means host RAM**.\n\nHere's the arithmetic. One sample is `ts` (13,2,4096,4096) fp32 = **1.74 GB** plus `forecast` (13,1,4096,4096) = **0.87 GB** \u2192 2.6 GB. At `batch_size: 2` that's 5.2 GB per batch. Your cell 11 passes `num_workers=4`, and `builders.py:145-153` sets `pin_memory=True` + `persistent_workers=True` with PyTorch's default `prefetch_factor=2`. So: 4 workers \u00d7 2 prefetched batches \u00d7 5.2 GB \u2248 **42 GB of pinned, non-swappable host RAM**, and the val loader keeps its own 4 persistent workers on top.\n\n## Two edits before you re-run\n\n**Cell 11** \u2014 change `num_workers=4` \u2192 `num_workers=0`. This kills the prefetch queue entirely (one batch resident instead of eight). S3 fetches now block the training loop, but with `max_samples: 10` and a warm cache that's a minor cost versus dying.\n\n**`configs/config_script.yaml`** \u2014 `batch_size: 1`. Halves everything again, on both host and GPU.\n\n## Run order, fresh kernel\n\n| Cell | Run? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2, 3 | \u2705 | Must be first \u2014 both set env vars that torch/cuBLAS read once at import |\n| 5 | \u2705 | Picks up your new `batch_size: 1` |\n| 7 | \u2705 | Cheap \u2014 assets are on disk, it just verifies |\n| 10, 11 | \u2705 | **11 with `num_workers=0`** |\n| **13** | \u274c **skip** | Pulls a full batch from S3 to print shapes you already confirmed (`ts (2,13,2,4096,4096)`, `forecast (2,13,1,4096,4096)`). Worse: it leaves that batch resident in the kernel for the rest of the session |\n| 15, 17 | \u2705 | |\n| 19 | \u2705 | Expect `157 / 159` |\n| 21 | \u2705 | Check it prints **~5.3 M** trainable |\n| **23** | \u274c **skip** | The expensive one. Full 4096 forward **on CPU**, and it parks an 872 MB `output` plus another batch in RAM for the whole fit. You already proved the shape is right |\n| 25, 26 | \u2705 | Cheap, and 39 needs the metric objects |\n| **28, 30, 32** | \u274c **skip** | They need `batch`/`output` from 13/23. Already verified: `mse 1.1731`, `rrse 1.1868` |\n| 34, 35, 37, 39 | \u2705 | All cheap |\n| 41, 43, 45 | \u2705 | |\n\nSkipping 13/23/28/30/32 is what buys you the headroom \u2014 those five cells alone were holding roughly 8 GB of host RAM that `trainer.fit` then had to work around. They're verification cells, and the verification already passed.\n\nOptional sanity check right before cell 45: `!free -g` \u2014 if `available` is under ~20 GB you're already starting in a hole and should restart the kernel.\n\nIf it still dies after these two changes, then it *is* the GPU, and you'll get a real `OutOfMemoryError` traceback instead of a dead kernel. That's a different fix.",
      "time": 1789588362.3385696,
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "527bb474-d901-4705-8d4d-8d51b3075080"
    },
