    {
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789591304.6575623,
      "id": "9519246c-64a9-46c0-8738-11d53acb30ea",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Short version: **it didn't predict a flare, and as configured it can't.** Nothing in this notebook has a flare output.\n\n## Where the flare label went\n\n`flare_dataset.py:142` computes `sample[\"ground_truth\"]` \u2014 the normalized log\u2081\u2080 GOES intensity, the `(2,)` scalar you saw in cell 13. But your `Flare2DLightningModule._target` returns `batch[\"forecast\"]`, which is imagery. So the flare label is loaded, collated, shipped to the GPU, and **discarded**. It appears in exactly one place: `label=...` in your plot title.\n\nYour model's task is: *given SDO at t\u221260 and t\u221236, reconstruct all 13 channels at t+0.* That's image regression. \"Did it predict a flare\" is a question you can only answer by looking at the flare-sensitive channels in those images.\n\n## Check this line in your own printout first\n\n```\n    offset from peak_time: ...\n```\n\nYour config has **`ds_time_tolerance: 4d`** with `ds_match_direction: forward`. Four days. So the `GOES_class` in your title can belong to a flare up to 96 hours *after* the imagery you're looking at. If that gap prints as hours or days, there is no flare anywhere in your ground-truth frame \u2014 the model had nothing to predict, and asking whether it \"got the flare\" is meaningless for that sample. Tighten to `15min` if you want samples where the GT frame actually contains the event.\n\n## Why none of the current numbers can see a flare\n\n| number | what it is | what it tells you about flares |\n|---|---|---|\n| `mse_model`, `train_loss`, `val_loss` | MSE in normalized (z-scored signum-log) space, dimensionless, averaged over **436 M values** | ~nothing |\n| `skill vs persistence` | vs. copying the \"now\" frame forward 36 min | whole-disk reconstruction only |\n| `rrse` (wandb) | <1 = beats predicting the channel mean | very low bar \u2014 most of a 4096\u00b2 frame is near-constant off-disk sky |\n| per-channel RMSE | DN/s for AIA, Gauss for HMI | whole-disk |\n\nA flare is a compact bright source, maybe 100\u00d7100 px out of 16.7 M per channel \u2014 **0.06% of the frame**. Averaging over the whole disk means a complete miss on the flare moves your MSE by less than the rounding in `0.940`. The loss is dominated by quiet Sun and background.\n\n## Add this to actually answer the question\n\nPaste after section 3 of the plot cell (it reuses `ins_phys` / `tgts_phys` / `prds_phys`):\n\n```python\n# ============================================================\n# DID IT GET THE FLARE?  Whole-disk MSE cannot tell you: the flare is\n# ~0.06% of the pixels. Score the flare region specifically.\n# ============================================================\nFLARE_CH = \"aia131\"                 # Fe XXI ~10 MK -- essentially only lights up in flares\nB = max(1, 256 // STRIDE)           # crop half-width, in displayed pixels\n\nn     = channel_order.index(FLARE_CH)\ngt_f  = tgts_phys[0][n]\npr_f  = prds_phys[0][n]\npe_f  = ins_phys[-1][n]             # persistence = the \"now\" frame, unchanged\n\n# The HEK catalog has no coordinates (only start/peak/end, GOES_class, AR), so\n# locate the brightening from the GROUND TRUTH itself.\niy, ix = np.unravel_index(np.argmax(gt_f), gt_f.shape)\nsl = (slice(max(iy - B, 0), iy + B), slice(max(ix - B, 0), ix + B))\n\nprint(f\"FLARE CHECK -- {FLARE_CH} | {row['GOES_class']} | AR {row['AR']}\")\nprint(f\"  brightest GT pixel (y={iy}, x={ix}); box = {(2*B)**2 / gt_f.size:.3%} of frame\")\nprint(f\"  {'':<20} {'ground truth':>13} {'prediction':>13} {'persistence':>13}\")\nfor label, f in [(\"peak, whole disk\", lambda a: a.max()),\n                 (\"peak, in box\",     lambda a: a[sl].max()),\n                 (\"mean, in box\",     lambda a: a[sl].mean())]:\n    print(f\"  {label:<20} {f(gt_f):>13.4g} {f(pr_f):>13.4g} {f(pe_f):>13.4g}\")\n\n_r = lambda a, b, s=None: float(np.sqrt(((a[s] - b[s]) ** 2).mean())) if s \\\n     else float(np.sqrt(((a - b) ** 2).mean()))\nfor tag, s in [(\"in box   \", sl), (\"full disk\", None)]:\n    rm, rp = _r(pr_f, gt_f, s), _r(pe_f, gt_f, s)\n    print(f\"  RMSE {tag}  model {rm:>11.4g}   persistence {rp:>11.4g}   skill {1 - rm/rp:+.1%}\")\n```\n\n**Reading it:**\n\n- **`peak, in box` for ground truth vs persistence** \u2014 if GT is much brighter, a real brightening happened in those 36 minutes. If they're similar, this sample has no flare evolution and there's nothing to grade.\n- **`peak, in box` for prediction vs ground truth** \u2014 the actual answer. Tracking GT and beating persistence means it anticipated the brightening.\n- **`skill in box` vs `skill full disk`** \u2014 if full-disk skill is decent but in-box skill is negative, the model reconstructs the quiet Sun fine and fails precisely where you care.\n\n## The caveat you should know before you interpret any of it\n\nExpect the prediction's peak to come out **well below** the ground truth's. That's not a bug \u2014 MSE's optimum is the *conditional mean*, so an MSE-trained image model systematically blurs away rare, compact, high-amplitude features. Flares are exactly that. You'd need a different loss (weighted toward high intensities, or adversarial/perceptual) to get sharp flare predictions out of this objective.\n\nWhich points at the real fork: if **flare forecasting** is the goal rather than image forecasting, that's `HelioSpectformer1D` regressing `batch[\"ground_truth\"]` \u2014 the original template path. The 2D setup you just built answers \"can Surya's backbone forecast the solar disk 36 minutes out,\" which is a legitimate and different question."
    },
