      "sender": "jpereira",
      "time": 1789592807.5896935,
      "body": "i see. thanks for the explanation. \n\nright now the purpose is to get image forecasting but good to know we can do flare forecasting too.\n\nim trying to come up with a plan for validation and how to best spend my time at this workshop. \nmy project involved confirming whether or not surya could predict what the future images will be regardless of whether the image stacks were flipped or not (this is because they trained it on last solar cycle but for the current solar cycle it was not predicting anything and the suspicion is that it might have to do with Hale's law for polarity in active regions, etc.). so i made relevant edits to config file and flare_dataset.py and now im trying to make relevant edits for when actually running surya and figuring out the actual plan for training/etc. \n\ni also changed the config edits so that we can look at differnt input intervals: surya originally was trained with 1 hr intervals and we'll do that but my personal task was looking at with smaller intervals (ie data is only available in 12 min chunks, so first input is 1 hr before ground truth, and then maybe 24 or 36 mins before ground truth as the second input, instead of 2 hrs before and 1 hr before ground truth). also we change the ground truth to peak flare time instead of start time so we aim to see whether image prediction will show how the flare looks. \n\nour metric here would just be how close the predicted is to the ground truth, which is why my image differences idea came from --i am just unsure how to completely account for this in the version of my notebook and work im doing in the notebook with running the prediction model \n\n\nwe were given these instructions:\n\"These are the recommendations I made of the kind of experiments you can run:\n- Runs with different amounts of training data and the same validation.\n- Runs with different training sets and the same validation (build your own ensemble).\n- Baseline and finetuning runs with identical training and validation sets.\n My recommendation is that you have a conversation with claude about a plan to run experiments that achieve any or several of this, given that you have about 36 hours (16 of which you\u2019ll be asleep) and training is slow. \"\n\nADDITION: i need to make sure that the model is working with flips and not flipped stacks because that's what we're checking with this project. AND the jobs ideally need to be under (and not close lol) 60 GB so it doesn't crash...",
      "id": "575555d6-b4e0-42d1-984d-39ff59356ec9",
      "type": "msg"
    },
