      "body": "I measured the data before planning, and a few things changed the shape of the answer:\n\n**Your validation set already contains the experiment.** Matching the flare catalog against the indices the way `flare_dataset.py` does gives **6,026 train samples** (cycle 24 only \u2014 the index skips 2022) and **299 val samples**, of which **97 are in 2023 (cycle 25, 9 M/X)** and **148 in 2014\u201315 (cycle-24 max)**. Train-on-24 / test-on-25 needs no new index building. Caveat: the catalog ends 2023-02-20, so \"current cycle\" means 2023 here, not 2025.\n\n**`peak_time` anchoring works** \u2014 the reference timestep lands a median **5\u20136 minutes** before each peak, so the target frame really is the flare. `ds_time_tolerance: 4d` looks alarming but only admits ~250 junk samples out of 6,279, because the dedupe already picks the nearest match. Not a blocker.\n\n**Three things I'd flag as real problems:**\n\n- **The decoder is never loaded.** The checkpoint contains `unembed.unembed.0.weight` at `(3328, 1280, 1, 1)` \u2014 bit-for-bit your 2D head \u2014 but `load_pretrained_weights` only tries `k` and `backbone.{k}`, so it's silently dropped and your head is still at random init. That's the `157 / 159`. Zero-shot evaluation of a random readout is meaningless, so this is a hard gate.\n- **`hmi_by` has the wrong sign under your flip.** Under y\u2192\u2212y a vector field goes `bx\u2192bx, by\u2192\u2212by, bz\u2192bz`. `flare_dataset.py:147-153` flips all 13 channels with no sign correction, so the mirrored stack isn't a physical mirror \u2014 and polarity is the whole point.\n- **`builders.py:104` hands the same `task_kwargs` to train *and* val**, so one call can't give you flipped-val and unflipped-train. Combined with `flip_probability` being random per-sample, you can't get a paired comparison. Fix: **flip in the eval loop, not the dataset** \u2014 one download, several forward passes, perfectly paired, no infra changes.\n\n**Also: downsampling is off the table.** `pooling` exists on the dataset, but `pos_embed` is `(1, 65536, 1280)` and both spectral filters are `(256, 129, 1280, 2)` \u2014 hard-wired to the 256\u00d7256 grid. Shrinking the image silently drops all three from the checkpoint.\n\n**The budget reframe.** Your measured rate is ~25\u201350 s per sample-pass, so ~1,500 train steps for the whole workshop \u2014 fine-tuning will barely move a 366M model. But *your actual question is about the pretrained model*, and forward-only eval is 3\u00d7 cheaper. So: zero-shot grid during the day (~2.5 h, and it's the real deliverable), the four training runs your advisor asked for overnight (~13 h in the 16-hour sleep window). The metric you're missing is `MSE(T(model(T(x))), model(x))` \u2014 if that's \u22480 the model is mirror-equivariant and Hale's law can't be the mechanism; if it's large, your hypothesis is live.\n\nPlan is written, including the per-sample metric table (with a persistence baseline and a flare-region-masked MSE, since a flare is ~0.06% of the disk and invisible in whole-disk MSE) and a memory recipe for the 60 GB ceiling.",
