            "raw_input": {
              "plan": "# Validation & experiment plan: does Surya forecast images across the Hale reversal?\n\n## Context\n\nThe 2D conversion of `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb` is **done and\nverified** (5.3 M trainable / 371 M total, `max_epochs=2 reached`, `train_loss 0.940` /\n`val_loss 0.938`, prediction-vs-truth-vs-difference plots working). This plan covers what to do\nwith the remaining ~20 usable hours of the workshop.\n\nThe scientific question is: **Surya was pretrained on cycle 24; it appears not to forecast\nusefully in cycle 25, and the suspected cause is Hale's polarity law.** A north\u2013south mirror of\nthe image is the cheap proxy for the cycle flip \u2014 mirroring a cycle-24 northern active region\ninto the south preserves the east\u2013west ordering of its bipole but moves it to a hemisphere where\ncycle 24 had the *opposite* leading polarity, i.e. it produces a configuration that looks like a\nvalid **cycle-25** region. That is why \"flipped vs unflipped\" is the right experiment.\n\nSecondary questions: shorter input spacing (`[-60, -36]` instead of Surya's ~2 h/1 h), and\n`peak_time` anchoring so the target frame is the flare itself.\n\n---\n\n## What the data actually allows (measured, not assumed)\n\nRun against `data/indices/surya_aws_s3_{train,val}.csv` and `data/hek_flare_catalog.csv`:\n\n| | matched samples | M/X | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| train index | **6,026** | 537 | years 2010\u20132021; **no 2022, no 2023** |\n| val index | **299** | 28 | years 2011\u20132023, **including 97 samples in 2023** |\n\n- **The val set already contains the experiment.** 2014+2015 \u2192 **148 cycle-24-maximum samples**;\n  **2023 \u2192 97 cycle-25 samples (9 M/X)**. Train is cycle 24 only. So train-on-24 / test-on-25 is\n  available with **zero new index building**.\n- The flare catalog **ends 2023-02-20** and the Surya index ends 2024-12-31 and skips 2022. So\n  \"current cycle\" means **2023** here, not 2025/26. Say so in the write-up.\n- **`peak_time` anchoring works as intended.** `flare_dataset.py:84-106` does\n  `merge_asof(direction=\"forward\")` then keeps the smallest `|delta|` per flare, so the reference\n  timestep is the Surya frame immediately *before* each peak \u2014 **median offset 5\u20136 minutes**. The\n  target frame really is the flare. `ds_time_tolerance: 4d` is *not* the disaster it looks like\n  (it only admits ~250 extra samples out of 6,279, because the dedupe already picks the nearest);\n  tightening to `12m` is a nice-to-have, not a blocker.\n- **Downsampling is impossible.** `HelioNetCDFDataset` has a `pooling` argument\n  (`helio.py:409, 908`), but the checkpoint's `embedding.pos_embed` is `(1, 65536, 1280)` and both\n  `blocks_spectral_gating.*.filter.complex_weight` are `(256, 129, 1280, 2)` \u2014 hard-wired to the\n  256\u00d7256 token grid. Shrinking the image silently drops the position embedding *and* both\n  spectral filters at load time. **Do not use `pooling`.**\n- **The warm cache does not help.** `/home/jovyan/scratch_space/surya_cache` holds 852 timestamped\n  files (467 GB, EFS, effectively unlimited space) but only **42** of them form a complete\n  `(-60, -36, 0)` triple. Nothing to exploit; but cache *does* persist across epochs, so epoch 2+\n  of any run is IO-free.\n- **Cost model.** The verified run did 5 train + 5 val steps at batch 2 in 7 m 58 s\n  \u21d2 **~25\u201350 s per sample-pass**, gradient-checkpointed on an L40S. Budget **~1,500 train-step\n  sample-passes for the entire remaining workshop.** Forward-only eval (`no_grad`, no optimizer\n  state) is ~3\u00d7 cheaper, ~10\u201315 s/sample.\n\n### The consequence that reframes everything\n\nFine-tuning at this budget will barely move a 366 M-parameter model. But **the question\n\"does Surya forecast images, flipped or not?\" is a question about the *pretrained* model \u2014 it\nneeds no training at all.** Zero-shot evaluation is 3\u00d7 cheaper per sample and answers the actual\nhypothesis. So: **spend the day on a zero-shot evaluation grid, spend the night on the four\ntraining runs your advisor asked for.**\n\n---\n\n## Blocker to fix first: the decoder is not being loaded\n\n`load_pretrained_weights` (`workshop_infrastructure/utils.py:404`) tries only the keys `k` and\n`backbone.{k}`. The 2D head's key is `head_unembed.unembed.0.weight`, so the checkpoint's\n`unembed.unembed.0.{weight,bias}` \u2014 shapes `(3328, 1280, 1, 1)` and `(3328,)`, where\n3328 = 16\u00b2\u00d713, i.e. **exactly our `LinearDecoder`** \u2014 is silently dropped. Cell 19 reports\n`157 / 159` and **the head stays at random init.**\n\nZero-shot evaluation of a random readout is meaningless, so this must be fixed before Tier 1.\n\n- **Do it notebook-locally** (respects \"notebook only\"): a new cell **between cells 19 and 21**,\n  before PEFT renames anything, that loads those two tensors into `model.head_unembed` by name\n  with a shape assertion. Re-check: the count should become **159 / 159** equivalent.\n- The clean fix is one line in `utils.py` \u2014 `for candidate in (k, f\"backbone.{k}\", f\"head_{k}\"):`,\n  guarded by the existing shape check so it cannot affect the 1D path. Worth a PR upstream, but\n  keep it out of this workshop's critical path unless you want to run\n  `pytest tests/test_lora_setup.py -v` to confirm nothing shifted.\n\n**Sanity gate:** with the decoder loaded, the *untrained* prediction panel must show a\nrecognizable Sun. If it is noise, stop \u2014 the load did not take.\n\n---\n\n## Design decision: flip in the evaluation loop, not in the dataset\n\n`flare_dataset.py:147-153` draws `np.random.rand() < flip_probability` per `__getitem__`. That is\nwrong for this experiment, three ways:\n\n- **It is random, so it is not paired.** You need the *same* validation sample scored both ways.\n- **`builders.py:104`** builds `common = {**_base_dataset_kwargs(...), **task_kwargs}` and hands\n  the *same* `task_kwargs` to both train and val, so one `build_helio_dataloaders` call can never\n  give you flipped-val and unflipped-train.\n- **`hmi_by` has the wrong sign.** Under y \u2192 \u2212y a vector field transforms as\n  `bx \u2192 bx`, `by \u2192 \u2212by`, `bz \u2192 bz`. The code flips all 13 channel arrays with no sign correction,\n  so the mirrored stack is not a physical mirror \u2014 and polarity is the entire point of the\n  experiment. `hmi_m`, `hmi_bz`, `hmi_v` are line-of-sight and correctly unchanged; only\n  **`hmi_by` (channel index 10)** needs negating.\n\n**Instead: keep `flip_probability=0.0` and apply the transforms explicitly in the eval loop.** One\ndownloaded batch, several forward passes, perfectly paired, zero dataset or infra changes \u2014 and it\nunlocks the metric you actually want (below). Define in the notebook:\n\n```\nT_id(x)       = x\nT_mirror(x)   = flip(x, dim=-2)                       # geometry only (what the dataset does today)\nT_hale(x)     = flip(x, dim=-2), then negate hmi_by    # physically correct mirror\nT_polinv(x)   = negate hmi_m, hmi_bx, hmi_by, hmi_bz   # pure cycle-24 \u2192 cycle-25 polarity reversal\n```\n\n**Sign flips happen in normalized space**, and the pipeline is signum-log *then* z-score, so the\nexact operation is `z \u2192 \u2212z \u2212 2\u03bc/\u03c3`, not `z \u2192 \u2212z`. Checked the scalers: for `hmi_m/bx/by/bz`,\n`2\u03bc/\u03c3 \u2264 0.006`, so plain negation is accurate to <0.6 % of a standard deviation \u2014 fine, but\ninclude the `\u22122\u03bc/\u03c3` term since the constants are already in `cfg`/`scalers`. Never negate an AIA\nchannel (`2\u03bc/\u03c3 = 2.41` for `aia171`, and intensities are not signed).\n\nEvery `T` here is an involution, so `T\u207b\u00b9 = T`.\n\nAlso note `HelioNetCDFDataset` has its **own** `random_vert_flip` (`helio.py:410, 650-653`) at a\nhardcoded p=0.5. `_base_dataset_kwargs` never forwards it so it is inert today \u2014 just don't turn\nit on, or you will get double-flipped samples.\n\n---\n\n## Metrics: what to record per sample\n\nWhole-disk MSE is dominated by quiet Sun, which is why the current `train_loss \u2248 val_loss \u2248 0.94`\ntells you almost nothing. Reuse `FlareMetrics` (`metrics/flare_metrics.py` \u2014 already flattens with\n`reshape(-1)`, so it works on images unchanged) but record a **row per sample** with:\n\n| column | why |\n|---|---|\n| `ds_index`, `year`, `GOES_class`, `CMX`, `cycle` (24 / 25) | the grouping axes |\n| `mse_id`, `mse_mirror`, `mse_hale`, `mse_polinv` | prediction vs. the *unflipped* ground truth, after mapping the prediction back with `T\u207b\u00b9` |\n| **`mse_persistence`** | copy the \"now\" frame `ts[:, :, -1]` forward. **This is the skill reference.** A forecast that cannot beat persistence at 36 minutes has no skill, no matter how good the picture looks. |\n| **`equivariance`** = `MSE(T(model(T(x))), model(x))` | **the central metric for your hypothesis.** \u22480 \u21d2 the model is mirror-equivariant, so flipping cannot help or hurt and Hale's law cannot be the mechanism. Large \u21d2 the model's answer genuinely depends on hemisphere/polarity, and the hypothesis is live. You do not have this number yet. |\n| `mse_flare_region` | MSE restricted to the top-0.1 % brightest pixels of the ground-truth `aia94`/`aia1600` frame. **This is how you answer \"did it predict the flare?\"** \u2014 a flare is ~0.06 % of the disk, so it is invisible in the whole-disk number. |\n\nThen the headline table is `groupby([\"cycle\", \"transform\"])` on skill score\n`1 \u2212 mse_model/mse_persistence`. Keep the existing input/truth/prediction/difference plot for 2\u20133\nhand-picked samples as the qualitative figure; the table is the result.\n\nPut these helpers in a **new file `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/evaluation.py`** (your own app\ndirectory, not shared infra) so the notebook and the overnight script use identical code.\n\n---\n\n## Tier 1 \u2014 zero-shot evaluation grid (~2.5 h, do this awake)\n\nNo training. `model.eval()`, `torch.no_grad()`, `batch_size=1`.\n\n- Val subsets, built by filtering the existing val dataset's `valid_indices` by year:\n  **cycle 24 = 2014+2015 (148 samples)**, **cycle 25 = 2023 (97 samples)**.\n- Transforms: `T_id`, `T_hale`, `T_polinv` (skip `T_mirror` \u2014 `T_hale` is the same geometry done\n  correctly; add `T_mirror` only if you want to quantify the `hmi_by` bug).\n- 245 samples \u00d7 3 forwards \u2248 735 passes \u2248 2.5 h.\n\n**This is the deliverable.** It answers, for the pretrained model: does it forecast at all (vs.\npersistence), is it worse in cycle 25 than cycle 24, and does mirroring or polarity-inverting the\ninput recover the cycle-25 performance.\n\n## Tier 2 \u2014 input-interval comparison (~1 h, optional)\n\n`[-60, -36]` (current) vs `[-60, -24]` vs `[-120, -60]` (Surya's pretraining spacing). Each needs\nits own dataset build because `valid_indices` depends on which offsets exist \u2014 so **intersect the\nthree `valid_indices` sets and evaluate only on the intersection**, or the comparison is confounded\nby different samples. Trim to ~60 samples per cycle \u00d7 2 transforms \u2248 240 passes \u2248 1 h.\n\nNote `[-120, -60]` is the fairest test of \"is Surya broken in cycle 25\", because it is the spacing\nthe checkpoint was trained on. If cycle-25 performance is bad even there, the interval change is\nnot the cause.\n\n## Tier 3 \u2014 four fine-tuning runs (~13 h, run overnight)\n\nIdentical val set (the 245 Tier-1 samples) for all four, so they are directly comparable. Batch 1,\nmatched **step count** (not epoch count) so data size is not confounded with training length.\n\n| run | train set | flip aug | answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** | 64 cycle-24 samples \u00d7 4 epochs = 256 steps | none | baseline vs. fine-tune on identical sets |\n| **B** | same 64 samples \u00d7 4 epochs | 50 % `T_hale` | does flip augmentation buy cycle-25 skill? |\n| **C** | 256 different cycle-24 samples \u00d7 1 epoch = 256 steps | none | **different amount of training data, same validation** |\n| **D** | 64 samples from *different years* \u00d7 4 epochs | none | **different training set, same validation \u2192 ensemble member with A** |\n\n\u22482.5 h each + ~50 min val each \u2248 13 h, inside the 16-hour sleep window with margin.\n\n- Run **B**'s train-time flip is the one place `flip_probability` is the right tool \u2014 but fix the\n  `hmi_by` sign first, and because `builders.py:104` shares `task_kwargs`, build the val loader\n  from a **second** `build_helio_dataloaders` call with `flip_probability=0.0`.\n- Wrap all four in one `nohup`'d shell script calling\n  `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/4_run_experiments.py` (new file, your app dir) so a crash in one\n  run does not cost you the night. Set `Trainer(max_time={\"hours\": 3})` and `ModelCheckpoint`\n  every epoch for the same reason.\n- **Fix the LoRA config before Tier 3.** `configs/config_script.yaml:148` has\n  `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]` \u2014 those are HuggingFace names.\n  Only `fc1`/`fc2` match anything in Surya, and PEFT raises only when *nothing* matches, so\n  **attention is silently never adapted**. Per `CLAUDE.md` it should be\n  `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Verify against the `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list printed at\n  startup; the trainable count will rise.\n\n## Memory recipe (stay well under 60 GB)\n\nThe earlier kernel death was **host RAM**, not GPU: 4 workers \u00d7 prefetch 2 \u00d7 5.2 GB/batch of\n`pin_memory=True` (non-swappable). `build_helio_dataloaders` does not expose `prefetch_factor`\n(defaults to 2) and sets `persistent_workers=True`, so workers are the knob.\n\n- `batch_size: 1` for eval, `1` for training. One `ts` is 1.7 GB, one 13\u00d74096\u00d74096 fp32\n  prediction is 0.87 GB.\n- `num_workers=0` for eval (proven safe), at most `2` for training.\n- Accumulate **scalars** into the results DataFrame; only materialize full images for the 2\u20133\n  samples you actually plot, and `del` them after.\n- Keep `bf16-mixed` and `checkpoint_layers: [0..9]`.\n- Watch it with `nvidia-smi` and `free -g` in a side terminal during the first run of each tier.\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\n1. **Decoder loaded** \u2014 the new cell between 19 and 21 reports both `unembed` tensors copied, and\n   the untrained prediction panel shows a recognizable Sun, not noise. Hard gate for Tier 1.\n2. **Transforms are involutions** \u2014 assert `T(T(x)) \u2248 x` for each `T` on one real batch\n   (`atol` a few times `2\u03bc/\u03c3`), and assert `T_hale` changes `hmi_by` but leaves `aia171` untouched\n   apart from the flip.\n3. **Sample counts** \u2014 the year-filtered val subsets have exactly **148** (2014+2015) and **97**\n   (2023) samples. Different numbers mean the tolerance or offsets changed.\n4. **Timing calibration first** \u2014 before committing to the schedule, time 10 eval samples and 10\n   train steps and rescale the whole plan from the measured rate. The 25\u201350 s/pass figure is\n   inferred from one progress bar; if it is 2\u00d7 worse, drop Tier 2 and cut Tier 3 to runs A and C.\n5. **Persistence sanity** \u2014 `mse_persistence` must be finite and the *same* for every transform\n   (it does not depend on the model). If it varies, the `T\u207b\u00b9` mapping is wrong.\n6. **Sample sets match across Tier 2** \u2014 `len(intersection)` printed, and every interval variant\n   evaluated on exactly that set.\n7. **Tier 3 comparability** \u2014 all four runs log the same `val_loss` key on the same 245 samples;\n   confirm identical `len(val_loader)` in each run's log.\n8. If you touch `utils.py` or the YAML: `pytest tests/test_lora_setup.py -v` still passes.\n\n## Expected outcomes, so you know what you are looking at\n\n- **`equivariance` \u2248 0** \u21d2 the model treats the mirrored Sun identically; Hale's law cannot explain\n  a cycle-25 failure, and the flip experiment is a null result (still publishable as a negative).\n- **`equivariance` large AND `mse_hale` < `mse_id` on cycle-25 samples** \u21d2 the headline positive\n  result: presenting cycle-25 regions in cycle-24 polarity convention recovers forecast skill.\n- **Nothing beats persistence anywhere** \u21d2 report that first and honestly. At a 36-minute horizon\n  persistence is very strong, and MSE's optimum is the conditional mean, so an MSE-trained image\n  model systematically blurs and under-predicts exactly the compact bright features a flare is.\n  That is a property of the loss, not a bug in your setup \u2014 and it is the reason\n  `mse_flare_region` is on the table above.\n",
              "planFilePath": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/i-need-help-changing-elegant-codd.md"
            },
