            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01E4kWHMHYFPMF4JaKYmw4PN",
            "raw_input": {
              "content": "# Workshop plan: does Surya forecast images equally well for both polarity orientations?\n\n## What you're testing, in one paragraph\n\nSurya was pretrained on one solar cycle. Hale's law says active-region polarity reverses from one\ncycle to the next, so a model that learned cycle 24's polarity convention may be useless on cycle\n25. You don't have two cycles of data, so you simulate the second one: take the data you have and\nvertically flip a random half of it, which produces the opposite polarity arrangement. Mix both\nversions through training, validation and testing. Then ask: **does the model do equally well on\nflipped and unflipped samples, or does it only work on the orientation it was pretrained on?**\n\nYour notebook can already produce predictions and difference plots. What it can't do yet is answer\nthat question, because it averages every sample into one `val_loss` number and throws away which\nsamples were flipped. Fixing that is the main work.\n\n---\n\n## Three things to fix before running anything\n\n### 1. The final layer of your model is untrained (this is the big one)\n\nYour model = pretrained Surya body + a small final layer that converts Surya's internal\nrepresentation back into a 13-channel image.\n\nThe checkpoint file contains a trained version of that final layer. But the loader looks for it\nunder the wrong name, doesn't find it, and skips it silently \u2014 that's the `157 / 159` you saw. So\n**your image-drawing layer starts as random numbers.** That's why the loss sat at 0.94 and hardly\nbudged: the body knows the Sun, the last step is guessing.\n\n**Fix:** one new cell, inserted **between cell 19 (load weights) and cell 21 (LoRA)** \u2014 it has to\ngo there, because cell 21 renames everything. The cell loads the checkpoint again and copies the\ntwo missing tensors into `model.head_unembed`, with a shape check so it fails loudly if the names\never change.\n\n**How you'll know it worked:** run the plotting cell on an *untrained* model. Before the fix, the\nprediction panel is static. After the fix, it looks like the Sun. That's your gate \u2014 don't run any\nexperiments until you see a Sun.\n\n### 2. The notebook can't tell flipped from unflipped results\n\nYou already set `was_flipped` on every sample. Nothing reads it.\n\n**Fix:** an evaluation cell that loops over the validation set one sample at a time and builds a\ntable \u2014 one row per sample, with these columns:\n\n| column | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| `timestamp`, `GOES_class` | which flare this is |\n| `was_flipped` | the grouping column \u2014 **this is the point** |\n| `error` | how far the prediction is from the truth (mean squared error) |\n| `error_persistence` | error you'd get by just copying the last input image forward and calling it the prediction |\n| `error_flare_region` | error computed only over the brightest 0.1% of pixels in the true image |\n\nThen `table.groupby(\"was_flipped\").mean()` is your result. Two rows. That's the experiment.\n\n**Why the extra two columns matter:**\n\n- **`error_persistence` is your reality check.** You're forecasting 36 minutes ahead. The Sun\n  barely changes in 36 minutes, so \"the next image looks like the last one\" is a *very* strong\n  guess. If your model can't beat it, the model isn't forecasting \u2014 it's copying. Every error\n  number you report should be shown next to this one, or it means nothing.\n- **`error_flare_region` is how you answer \"did it predict the flare?\"** A flare covers roughly\n  0.06% of the disk. Whole-image error is dominated by quiet Sun, so a model that completely misses\n  the flare and a model that nails it will have nearly identical whole-image error. Masking to the\n  brightest pixels is the only way to see it.\n\nUse `batch_size=1` for this loop. Then each row is unambiguously flipped or not, and memory stays\nlow.\n\n### 3. Score every validation image twice\n\nInstead of relying on the random flip to give you a balanced validation set, take each validation\nimage and score it **twice**: once upright, once flipped. Same image, both orientations, so the two\ngroups are made of the same solar conditions and any difference is due to orientation alone rather\nthan to which samples happened to get flipped.\n\nThis costs one extra forward pass per sample and needs no changes to `flare_dataset.py` or the\nconfig \u2014 the flip is two lines in the evaluation loop. Keep `flip_probability` for *training*\n(where random mixing is what you want) and do validation this way.\n\n---\n\n## What your data actually contains\n\nI measured this against your index files and flare catalog, because it sets every experiment size.\n\n- **6,026 usable training samples** and **299 usable validation samples** (a sample = a flare with\n  all three needed frames present).\n- **Your `peak_time` change works.** The target frame lands a median of **5\u20136 minutes before each\n  flare peak**. So the image the model is asked to predict really is the flare. Good.\n- `ds_time_tolerance: 4d` looks alarming but is nearly harmless \u2014 the matching code already picks\n  the nearest frame per flare, so 4 days only lets in ~250 junk samples out of 6,279. Tightening it\n  to `12m` is a small cleanup, not a blocker. Your call.\n- **Bonus you may not know about:** your validation set has **97 samples from 2023** \u2014 actual\n  cycle 25 \u2014 alongside **148 from 2014\u201315** (cycle 24 maximum). Your training data is cycle 24\n  only. So on top of the simulated-flip experiment, you can run the *real* thing for free: train on\n  cycle 24, compare error on the 2014\u201315 samples against the 2023 samples. Same evaluation table,\n  just group by year instead of by `was_flipped`. If the flip experiment and the real-cycle\n  comparison agree, that's a much stronger result than either alone.\n- **You cannot shrink the images to go faster.** The dataset has a `pooling` option, but Surya's\n  position embedding and both spectral filters are hard-wired to a 4096\u00d74096 input. Shrinking the\n  image would silently discard those pretrained pieces. Don't touch it.\n\n---\n\n## Time budget \u2014 the honest version\n\nFrom your one completed run (5 training + 5 validation steps at batch size 2 in 8 minutes), each\nsample costs roughly **25\u201350 seconds** to train on. Evaluation-only is about 3\u00d7 cheaper.\n\nThat means **one training run of a few hundred steps is about 3 hours**, and you have room for\nabout three or four of them plus evaluation. Fine-tuning will not transform a 366M-parameter model\nat this scale \u2014 so treat the trained runs as a comparison between conditions, not as an attempt to\nbuild a good forecaster.\n\n**Calibrate before committing:** time 10 evaluation samples and 10 training steps, then rescale\neverything below from what you actually measure. If it's 2\u00d7 slower than this estimate, drop to two\ntraining runs.\n\n---\n\n## The schedule\n\n### Now, ~1 hour \u2014 get the plumbing right\nFix the final layer (#1). Add the evaluation table cell (#2, #3). Smoke-test on 10 validation\nsamples with `max_samples: 10`. Confirm the untrained prediction looks like the Sun.\n\n### Next, ~1 hour \u2014 your first real result, no training required\nRun the evaluation table on ~100 validation samples, upright and flipped, using the **pretrained\nmodel with no fine-tuning at all**.\n\nThis is worth doing first because your central question \u2014 \"does Surya handle both orientations?\" \u2014\nis a question about the pretrained model, and answering it costs one hour instead of three.\nYou'll immediately know whether flipping hurts, and whether Surya beats persistence at all.\n\n### Overnight, ~13 hours \u2014 three training runs\nIdentical settings except the flip rate, all evaluated on the same validation set:\n\n| run | `flip_probability` during training | what it tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | `0.0` \u2014 one orientation only | baseline: the pretraining convention only |\n| **B** | `0.5` \u2014 mixed, your main experiment | can it learn both orientations at once? |\n| **C** | `1.0` \u2014 flipped only | can it learn the *other* orientation at all? |\n\nThen a fourth run if you have time: **run B again on a different set of training samples of the\nsame size**. Comparing those two gives you the \"different training sets, same validation\" /\nensemble experiment your advisor suggested, and tells you how much of any difference is just noise\nfrom which samples you happened to pick.\n\n**Practical protections for an unattended overnight job:**\n- Put the runs in a plain shell script launched with `nohup`, so a crash in run A doesn't cost you\n  runs B and C.\n- Give each `Trainer` a wall-clock cap (`max_time={\"hours\": 3}`) so no single run eats the night.\n- Save a checkpoint every epoch.\n- Keep `batch_size` at 1\u20132 and `num_workers` at 0\u20132. The crash you had earlier was **host RAM**,\n  not GPU \u2014 4 loader workers each holding two 5 GB batches in pinned memory. Two workers is the\n  ceiling here; the loader doesn't expose the prefetch depth, so workers are your only knob. Watch\n  it with `free -g` during the first run.\n\n### Morning, ~4 hours \u2014 analysis\nCombine the four tables. Two headline comparisons:\n1. `error` grouped by `was_flipped`, per run, always shown next to `error_persistence`.\n2. `error` grouped by year (2014\u201315 vs 2023), which is the real cycle comparison.\n\nPlus the input/truth/prediction/difference figure you already built, for two or three hand-picked\nM-class flares \u2014 one that worked and one that didn't.\n\n### One config fix before the overnight runs\n`configs/config_script.yaml` line 148 lists `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj,\nfc1, fc2]`. Four of those six names don't exist anywhere in Surya \u2014 they're from a different model\nlibrary. The LoRA code only complains if *nothing* matches, and `fc1`/`fc2` do match, so it runs\nhappily while **never adapting any attention layer**. The correct list for Surya is\n`[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Change it and the printed trainable-parameter count will go up \u2014\nthat's your confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## How to read the results\n\n- **Flipped and unflipped errors come out about equal, and both beat persistence** \u2192 the model\n  handles both polarity orientations fine. Hale's law is not the explanation for the cycle-25\n  problem, and you've ruled out a leading hypothesis. That's a real result.\n- **Flipped error is clearly worse than unflipped** \u2192 the model is tied to the orientation it was\n  pretrained on. This directly supports the Hale's-law explanation. Then run B vs. run A tells you\n  whether mixing both orientations during training fixes it.\n- **Run C (flipped-only training) does as well as run A** \u2192 the model can learn either orientation\n  fine, it just can't hold both at once. Different problem, still interesting.\n- **Nothing beats persistence anywhere** \u2192 say so first and plainly. At a 36-minute horizon\n  persistence is genuinely hard to beat. Also worth knowing: mean-squared-error training pushes a\n  model toward the *average* likely image, which systematically blurs out small bright features \u2014\n  exactly what a flare is. So a blurry, flare-free prediction is partly a property of the loss\n  function, not a bug in your setup. That's why `error_flare_region` is in the table.\n\n---\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n1. Untrained prediction looks like the Sun, not static \u2192 the final-layer fix took.\n2. Flipping a sample twice returns the original (`flip(flip(x)) == x`) \u2192 your flip is a clean\n   involution and the eval loop isn't corrupting anything.\n3. `error_persistence` is identical for the upright and flipped scoring of the same sample \u2014 it\n   doesn't involve the model, so if it differs, the flip is being applied inconsistently.\n4. The validation set has 299 samples; the year subsets have 148 (2014\u201315) and 97 (2023). Different\n   numbers mean the tolerance or the input offsets changed.\n5. All runs report the same validation-set size in their logs, or they aren't comparable.\n6. `free -g` stays well under 60 GB through the first run of each type.\n",
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/i-need-help-changing-elegant-codd.md"
            },
            "kind": "edit",
