      "body": "Rewritten in plain language. The short version:\n\n**Fix three things first (~1 hour):**\n1. Your model's final image-drawing layer is currently random numbers \u2014 the checkpoint has a trained one but the loader misses it. One cell between cells 19 and 21. Test: an untrained prediction should look like the Sun, not static.\n2. Add an evaluation cell that builds a table with one row per validation sample, including the `was_flipped` flag you already record. Right now everything collapses into one `val_loss`, so the notebook literally cannot show you the flipped-vs-unflipped difference. `groupby(\"was_flipped\")` on that table *is* your experiment.\n3. Score each validation image twice \u2014 once upright, once flipped \u2014 so both groups contain the same solar conditions.\n\n**Two columns in that table earn their keep:** a persistence baseline (just copy the last input image forward), because 36 minutes ahead is an easy interval and you need to know if the model beats \"nothing changed\"; and error over only the brightest 0.1% of pixels, because a flare is 0.06% of the disk and is invisible in whole-image error.\n\n**Then:** one hour of evaluation on the *pretrained* model gives you a first answer with no training at all. Overnight, three ~3-hour training runs at `flip_probability` 0.0 / 0.5 / 1.0, same validation set. Morning for analysis.\n\n**Two bonuses from measuring your data:** your `peak_time` change works \u2014 the target frame lands 5\u20136 minutes before each flare peak. And your validation set already has 97 samples from 2023 (real cycle 25) versus 148 from 2014\u201315 (cycle 24), while your training data is cycle 24 only. So you can run the *real* cycle comparison alongside the simulated flip one, using the same table grouped by year instead. If both point the same way, that's a much stronger result.\n\nOne config line needs changing before the overnight runs: `target_modules` lists four layer names that don't exist in Surya, so Lo