    {
      "id": "4fa9478f-f2a9-4f81-8ca9-22fe1a14de65",
      "time": 1789599173.1788702,
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Good \u2014 let's stay in words. No approval request in this message.\n\n## Why your prediction looks foggy and dim\n\nThis one has a satisfying answer, and your own numbers confirm it.\n\nInside the pipeline, images are normalized so each channel has **mean 0 and standard deviation 1**. So if a model just predicted \"zero everywhere\" \u2014 i.e. the average Sun, no features \u2014 its mean-squared error would be almost exactly **1.0**.\n\nYour run reported **0.940**. Random initialization gave **1.17**.\n\nRead those three numbers together: your model is sitting a hair below \"predict the average and give up.\" It has learned to output something very close to a featureless average image, with a whisper of structure on top. A featureless average image, when you plot it, is exactly **foggy and dim**. The fog isn't a rendering artifact \u2014 it's the model genuinely having almost nothing to say.\n\nAnd yes, that's the missing-final-layer problem. Two contributing effects, in order of size:\n\n1. **The untrained final layer** (the big one). A randomly-initialized final layer produces small, near-zero-mean values with no meaningful channel structure. Everything lands near the channel average \u2192 gray haze.\n2. **Mean-squared error inherently blurs** (a smaller, permanent effect). MSE is minimized by predicting the *average of all plausible futures*. Averaging over possible futures smears out small bright features. So even a perfectly trained MSE model gives you somewhat soft images. This one you can't fix, only be aware of \u2014 it's why I want a flare-region-only error column.\n\nEffect 1 explains most of your fog and goes away when we load the weights. Effect 2 stays, and is why \"predicting the flare sharply\" is genuinely hard.\n\n## What \"the final layer is untrained\" means \u2014 and no, it's not your research question\n\nIt's a plumbing bug, not a finding. Nothing to discover; just something to fix.\n\nYour model is two pieces bolted together:\n\n- **The Surya body** \u2014 pretrained, knows the Sun, works fine. It reads your input images and produces an internal numerical summary: 65,536 patches \u00d7 1,280 numbers each. Not a picture. Its own internal shorthand.\n- **The final layer** \u2014 a translator that converts that shorthand back into a 13-channel 4096\u00d74096 image.\n\nThe checkpoint file on disk **contains a trained translator.** But the checkpoint calls it `unembed`, and your model calls it `head_unembed` \u2014 the `head_` prefix is required by the LoRA machinery so the layer stays trainable. The loading code tries the plain name, and tries adding `backbone.`, but never tries adding `head_`. So it doesn't find it, skips it, and doesn't warn you. 157 tensors load, 2 get dropped. That's the `157 / 159`.\n\nNet effect: **an expert scientist paired with a translator who is babbling.** Fixing it costs one cell and gives you a working forecaster to *start* your experiment from \u2014 which matters, because \"does flipping hurt?\" is only a meaningful question about a model that works in the first place.\n\n## Flipped vs. not-flipped metrics\n\nAgreed, and I want to be precise about which thing we're doing, because \"different\" could mean two things and both are covered:\n\n- **The mechanism:** report them as two separate numbers, never one blended average. During training you'd log `val_loss_upright` and `val_loss_flipped`. After training, a table with one row per sample and a `was_flipped` column, so you can group it.\n- **The result:** whether those two numbers actually come out different is your finding. Equal \u2192 the model handles both polarity orientations, Hale's law isn't the culprit. Flipped clearly worse \u2192 it's locked to the orientation it was pretrained on, which supports your hypothesis.\n\nRight now the notebook computes one averaged `val_loss`, so it structurally cannot show you either outcome.\n\n## How much data, and how long \u2014 the exchange rate\n\nHere's the part that was missing. From your completed run, two rates:\n\n| | cost |\n|---|---|\n| **Training** on one sample (see it once) | ~35 seconds |\n| **Evaluating** one sample (no training) | ~12 seconds |\n\nWhich converts to:\n\n> **1 hour of training \u2248 100 sample-views.** &nbsp;&nbsp;\u2022&nbsp;&nbsp; **1 hour of evaluating \u2248 300 samples.**\n\n(\"Sample-view\" = one sample seen once. 150 samples \u00d7 2 epochs = 300 sample-views = 3 hours.)\n\nAgainst that, what you *have*: **6,026 training samples, 299 validation samples.**\n\nSo a realistic shape:\n\n| activity | data used | epochs | sample-views | wall clock |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Evaluate the pretrained model, both orientations | 100 val samples \u00d7 2 | \u2014 | \u2014 | **~40 min** |\n| One training run | 150 train samples | 2 | 300 | **~3 h** |\n| Evaluate that run, both orientations | 100 val samples \u00d7 2 | \u2014 | \u2014 | **~40 min** |\n| \u2192 **per run, all-in** | | | | **~3.7 h** |\n| Three runs (flip rate 0.0 / 0.5 / 1.0) | | | | **~11 h** |\n\nThat fits your overnight window. And now the honest part:\n\n**150 samples is 2.5% of your training data.** That's not a choice, it's what the clock allows. Consequences worth accepting up front:\n\n- Don't expect fine-tuning to transform the model. The three runs are a **controlled comparison between conditions at equal tiny budget** \u2014 \"does mixing orientations help, holding everything else fixed?\" \u2014 not an attempt to build a good forecaster.\n- **The evaluation of the pretrained model is your most robust result**, precisely because it doesn't depend on the training budget at all. It costs 40 minutes and it directly answers your core question. That's why I want it done first, before any training.\n\nOne tradeoff to decide: at a fixed 300 sample-views, you can do 150 samples \u00d7 2 epochs or 300 samples \u00d7 1 epoch. More distinct samples usually generalizes better, but with only one pass the model may not have converged. My lean: **300 \u00d7 1**, and watch whether the training loss is still falling at the end \u2014 if it is, you were compute-limited, and that's worth reporting.\n\n## On \"testing\" \u2014 you asked, and I hadn't addressed it\n\nYou have two index files (train and validation), not three. But you can get a clean three-way split for free, because I found something in your validation set:\n\n- **148 samples from 2014\u201315** (cycle 24 maximum)\n- **97 samples from 2023** (actual cycle 25)\n- and your training data is **cycle 24 only**\n\nSo: use ~100 of the 2014\u201315 samples as your **validation** set (the one you look at repeatedly while tuning), and hold the **97 samples from 2023 as a test set you touch exactly once, at the very end.** That gives you the real cycle comparison \u2014 train on cycle 24, test on cycle 25 \u2014 running alongside your simulated flip experiment. If the flip result and the real-cycle result point the same direction, that's a far stronger claim than either alone.\n\n---\n\nTwo things I'd like your read on before I touch anything:\n\n1. Does the \"1 hour \u2248 100 training samples\" framing give you what you needed on the data/time question, or do you want it broken down differently?\n2. Are you happy holding the 2023 samples back as a one-shot test set, or would you rather see them from the start? Holding them back is better practice but means you're partly flying blind until the end.",
      "mentions": [],
      "type": "msg"
    }
